# Expectation record for 03_initializers/011_flexible_array_member.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 011_flexible_array_member
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Flexible array member declaration, sizing and ELEMENT ACCESS through the member itself: an object allocated as the header plus FAM_CAP elements, whose every live element is written and read as p->data[i] and summed across a function boundary that knows only the incomplete type, alongside allocated-equivalent storage at BOTH storage durations -- a union pairing the incomplete-array struct with a same-prefix sized struct, once as a file-scope static image and once as an automatic twin inside main, with the shared count written through one arm and read back through the other
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour, which is the precondition both
differential oracles rest on: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do anything at
all, so a divergence between two compilers over it would prove nothing about either one. The storage
technique below was chosen precisely to keep that true.
THE STANDARD'S OWN IDIOM IS USED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ELEMENT ACCESS WELL DEFINED. C11
6.7.2.1p18 sizes the replacement array against THE OBJECT BEING ACCESSED, and 6.7.2.1p20's EXAMPLE 2
shows the construction that makes that object large enough: an allocation of sizeof(struct fam) plus
room for n elements, after which the member behaves as if it had n elements. fam_alloc performs
exactly that with n == FAM_CAP == 8, so `p->data[i]` for i below the object's own count is inside the
replacement array by the standard's own account. Two allocated-EQUIVALENT objects are held beside it,
at STATIC and AUTOMATIC storage duration, because a compiler can get a data-section image right and
the emitted stores wrong; those two are unions and their elements are reached through the sized arm
where they are declared, while only the member the two arms SHARE is reached through the flexible arm.
THE ALLOCATOR IS DECLARED WITHOUT A HEADER AND WITHOUT LOSING A TARGET. The corpus rule is that a
program includes no header and hand-declares its libc prototypes, and the obstacle was that malloc
takes size_t, which is unsigned int on i686 and unsigned long on the other three targets, so no single
spelling serves all four and an unprototyped declaration is refused by the gate. The size type is
therefore selected by the architecture macro the compiler predefines -- __i386__/__i386 giving
unsigned int, and __x86_64__/__amd64__/__aarch64__/__arm64__/__riscv/__riscv__ giving unsigned long --
with an #error on the unrecognised branch so a fifth target fails to TRANSLATE rather than inheriting a
wrong argument width. This is the same architecture-macro idiom 12_preprocessor/004_predefined_macros.c
and 10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c already use. MEASURED: the declaration matches the
built-in exactly on all four reference drivers, so the gate raises no
-Werror=builtin-declaration-mismatch, at -O0, -O1 and -O2.
HERMETICITY IS UNAFFECTED BY THE ALLOCATION. An allocation opens no file, no socket and no path; it
reads nothing from the environment, the clock or the file system. The single block obtained is released
with free before main returns, so the program owns no storage at exit and the address sanitizer's leak
checker is satisfied -- MEASURED clean under -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all,
which built, ran, exited 0 and printed nothing on standard error.
ALLOCATION FAILURE IS HANDLED DETERMINISTICALLY RATHER THAN ASSUMED AWAY. The request is 36 bytes, and
a refusal would make every subsequent element access a null dereference, so the result is checked. On
refusal the program prints the single fixed line alloc_refused=1 and returns 3 -- inside the suite's
0-125 range -- so even that path is byte-deterministic and would surface as an exit-code divergence
rather than as a crash. The golden record below is the success path, which is what a 36-byte request
takes on every target measured here.
ALIASING IS THE HAZARD IN THIS CONSTRUCT, AND IT IS MADE LEGAL BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY HOPE.
A bare `static int backing[N]` cast to `struct fam *` WOULD be a strict-aliasing violation -- the
storage would be written as an int array and read through an unrelated struct type -- so that
spelling is not used. Instead both types are members of the SAME union: C11 6.5.2.3p6 permits
inspecting the common initial sequence of structures that share a union, which here is the leading
`int count`, and the two data arrays begin at the same offset with the same element type, so every
access reads or writes storage last written through an lvalue of that same type. No pointer cast
appears anywhere in the program.
The flexible array member itself contributes nothing to sizeof the struct (C11 6.7.2.1p18), which is
asserted directly rather than relied on silently: sizeof_struct_fam is printed and must read 4, and
fam_size_as_if_omitted asserts the inequality the standard actually states -- the size is as if the
member were omitted, allowing only extra trailing padding -- against a header-only twin type.
WHICH SUBSCRIPT GOES THROUGH WHICH VIEW, STATED EXACTLY, BECAUSE THAT IS THE PROGRAM'S CENTRAL SAFETY
PROPERTY. C11 6.7.2.1p18 replaces the member with the longest array that would not make the structure
larger than THE OBJECT BEING ACCESSED, and states that if that array would have no elements, any
attempt to access an element is undefined. Two consequences follow and both are honoured. A
`struct fam *` aimed at a UNION arm designates an object of size sizeof(struct fam) -- four bytes, the
header alone -- so the replacement array there has NO elements: the union's elements are therefore
never subscripted through the flexible arm, only through the SIZED arm whose data member is declared
with FAM_CAP == 8 elements, and the flexible arm of a union is used only for the member the two arms
share. The ALLOCATED object is the opposite case by construction: fam_alloc obtained
sizeof(struct fam) + FAM_CAP * sizeof(int) bytes, so its replacement array has FAM_CAP elements and
`ap->data[i]` is in bounds for every i below FAM_CAP. Every subscript on `struct fam::data` in this
program is on that object.
EVERY SUBSCRIPT IS IN BOUNDS, AND EVERY BOUND IS DERIVED FROM THE OBJECT RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
sum_prefix loops to u->flex.count -- the shared member, read through the flexible arm -- and indexes
u->sized.data, so the bound comes from the object and the storage comes from the arm that declares it;
fam_sum and every read loop over the allocated object loop to p->count, likewise taken from the object.
The static union's count is only ever 5 then 6 and the highest index it touches is 5; the automatic
union's count is only ever 4 then 5 and the highest index it touches is 4; the allocated object's count
is only ever 0, then 5, then FAM_LIVE_MAX == 6, and the highest index it touches is 5. Every one of
those counts is strictly below the capacity of 8, so no access reaches past the storage, no
one-past-the-end pointer is formed or dereferenced, and no pointer arithmetic leaves its object. `p`
designates the file-scope object, which lives for the whole run; `lp` designates the automatic object,
read only inside main where it is alive; `ap` designates the allocated object, which is created before
its first use and freed after its last, so none of the three pointers is ever dangling and the freed
pointer is never used again.
NO UNINITIALIZED STORAGE IS READ, at any of the three storage classes. Both unions carry a designated
initializer for their sized arm, so every element of that arm is determinate -- the live values plus
the explicit trailing zeros -- and a member or element an initializer had omitted would be zeroed in
any case, for automatic and static duration alike (C11 6.7.9p19 and p21). The static object is
additionally covered by C11 6.7.9p10, which defines a static-duration object without an initializer as
zero, but the automatic twin does not rely on any such pre-zeroing: its whole image is established by
emitted stores on entry to the block, which is the very difference the twin exists to test. The
ALLOCATED object receives no such guarantee from the standard -- malloc leaves its bytes indeterminate
-- so fam_alloc writes all FAM_CAP elements to zero and the header before returning, and fam_fill then
overwrites the live prefix; the one slot the mutation phase newly brings inside the count, index 5, is
written before it is read. No element of that object is ever read before it has been written.
No signed overflow is possible: the largest printed value is 2133, the largest element is 999, and the
largest sum over the allocated object is 1187, while INT_MAX is at least 2147483647 on every conforming
target. fam_fill computes first + i * step with first == 7, step == 11 and i below 5, so its largest
product is 44. There is no shift, no division, no remainder and no integer-to-pointer conversion
anywhere in the program.
No object is modified twice between two sequence points. Each of the three mutation phases is three
separate statements -- one element rewritten in place, the count grown, one previously zero slot filled
-- and the accumulation inside sum_prefix, fam_sum and fam_fill is a statement of its own. Every printf
call receives at most one loop counter and one already-computed value, so the unspecified order in which
a function call's arguments are evaluated cannot be observed.
No object representation is inspected and nothing is read byte by byte, so neither padding nor byte
order can influence the output, and no address is printed -- neither the allocated block's, whose value
would differ between runs and between targets, nor either union view's, which are reached only through
int-typed lvalues at identical offsets. Nothing depends on the relative addresses of unrelated objects:
no pointer difference is taken, no two addresses are compared, and the only pointer comparison anywhere
is the allocation's own result against a null pointer constant.
Every conversion specifier is %d and every corresponding argument has type int after the default
argument promotions -- including both sizeof results, each cast to int before reaching the variadic
call so that no wider type and no size_t is passed -- so no variadic argument is read at the wrong
width.
main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated by the
operating system. Every input is a literal in this file: nothing is read from the environment, the
clock, the file system or the network, and iteration order is fixed by counts the program itself
printed, so every cell is reproducible from this source and this record alone.
No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard
I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so naming one would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a)
while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by a
compiler. This area is granted no header exception; the corpus has exactly two, area 07's stdarg.h
and the dedicated bundled-header probe in area 12.
This record declares no warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none. The program is clean
under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
-Werror in the single native compile-only invocation the suite's warning gate makes, and it builds
and runs clean under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both
gates are reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag
discipline is untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows ONE thing and records the reason below, as the suite requires: one SPELLING of the
initializer. It narrows no target, no optimization level and no oracle, it carries no
expected-divergence marker, and no `ub_audit_flags` key appears because the program passes the suite's
full default warning gate unmodified.
ELEMENT ACCESS THROUGH THE FLEXIBLE MEMBER IS NO LONGER EXCLUDED, AND THIS PARAGRAPH RECORDS WHAT
CHANGED, BECAUSE AN EARLIER FORM OF THIS RECORD EXCLUDED IT. The exclusion rested on two claims. The
first was sound and still is: a `struct fam *` aimed at a UNION arm designates a four-byte object, so
under C11 6.7.2.1p18 its replacement array has no elements and subscripting it would be undefined --
which is why the union's elements are still reached only through the sized arm. The second claim was
that the construction the standard does support, an allocated object genuinely larger than the header,
was out of reach for this corpus, because a declaration of an allocation function needs size_t, which
is `unsigned int` on i686 and `unsigned long` on the other three targets, so no single spelling serves
all four, and because an unprototyped declaration was MEASURED to be rejected by the reference compiler
and the alternate reference compiler alike (-Werror=builtin-declaration-mismatch and
-Werror=strict-prototypes respectively). That second claim is now superseded by measurement: the size
type does not have to be one spelling. Selecting it with the architecture macro the compiler
predefines -- the same idiom 12_preprocessor/004_predefined_macros.c and
10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c already rely on, with an #error on the unrecognised
branch -- yields a prototype that matches each target's own size_t exactly, and it was MEASURED clean
under the full default gate with all four reference drivers at -O0, -O1 and -O2, with no
built-in-declaration-mismatch diagnostic and with the sanitizer gate, leak check included, silent. So
the program now performs the C11 6.7.2.1p20 EXAMPLE-2 construction and subscripts the flexible member
on an object large enough for it, which is what the plan's entry for this program asks for when it
prescribes "declaration, sizing, and access". Nothing was traded away to get it: the union pair is
kept, at both storage durations, because it tests the common-initial-sequence access C11 6.5.2.3p6
sanctions, which an allocated object cannot.
ONE SPELLING OF THIS CONSTRUCT IS EXCLUDED, NARROWLY AND ON AN EXPLICIT PLAN BASIS RATHER THAN ON
DIFFICULTY. Direct initialization -- `static struct fam d = { 3, {1,2,3} };` -- was probed against
the mandatory strict gate in this environment and REJECTED with "error: initialization of a flexible
array member" under -Werror=pedantic. Unlike every other gate interaction in this feature area, this
one is NOT a style diagnostic reporting a well-defined construct: initializing a flexible array
member is a GNU EXTENSION rather than standard C, which is exactly why -pedantic -- whose entire
purpose is to reject extensions -- diagnoses it. It is the one place in this area where the gate is
reporting a real portability fact.
THE BASIS FOR EXCLUDING IT is the plan's own specification for this program, which PRESCRIBES the
technique to use, naming "flexible array member declaration, sizing, and access through
allocated-equivalent static storage", and which assigns GNU extensions to the 08_gcc_extensions
area, whose fixed program list this construct is not on. Area 03 sanctions no gate deviation, and
inventing one to admit a non-standard spelling would weaken requirement 1's machine half for every
other program in the area. Suppressing -pedantic here with a scoped pragma -- the mechanism used
elsewhere in this area for genuinely standard constructs -- would not merely relax a style check: it
would move the construct under test from standard C to a GNU extension while leaving it in an area
that does not test extensions, so the divergence surface would no longer be the one this program is
named for.
THE EXCLUSION IS SCOPED TO ONE WAY OF FILLING THE MEMBER AND COSTS NO COVERAGE OF THE FEATURE, and
it is recorded here rather than left to look like an oversight. Declaration of the incomplete last
member, its zero contribution to sizeof, ELEMENT ACCESS through the member itself on an object large
enough for it -- every live element written as ap->data[i], read back individually and summed across a
function boundary that knows only the incomplete type -- and mutation through both union arms are all
exercised. The initializer whose spelling is excluded would have written exactly the values fam_fill
now writes by assignment, so the excluded spelling costs the FEATURE nothing at all; and the two union
objects add the storage-duration axis, since g_store is a data-section image and l_store is
materialized by emitted stores on entry to main. A compiler can get the static image right and the
run-time stores wrong, so both paths are held side by side. Both techniques were probed the same way
and are gate-clean at -O0, -O1 and -O2 as well as sanitizer-clean.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Every value is an int printed with %d, and every printed size is cast to int
before it reaches the variadic call, so no size_t is ever passed. The only size_t-width expression in
the program is the argument of malloc, which is consumed by the allocator and never printed. sizeof is
never applied to a
pointer, and no long, ptrdiff_t, pointer, floating or character value appears anywhere, so neither
the MEASURED pointer width -- four bytes on i686 and eight on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64
(docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines 457-462) -- nor the MEASURED plain-char
signedness split, signed on x86_64 and i686 and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, can influence a byte
of stdout.
SIZES ARE TARGET-INVARIANT FOR A REASON THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. int is 4 bytes and
4-byte aligned on all four targets, so no padding sits between count and data in either union arm:
sizeof(struct fam) is 4, because the flexible array member contributes nothing to the size of the
struct (C11 6.7.2.1p18), and sizeof(union fam_storage) is 4 + 8 * 4 == 36, the size of its sized arm.
PADDING, BYTE ORDER AND ADDRESSES. No object representation is inspected and nothing is read byte by
byte, so neither padding nor endianness can influence the output, and no address is ever printed --
the union views are reached only through int-typed lvalues at identical offsets, and the allocated
block's address, which would differ between runs and between targets, is never converted or printed.
alloc_sizeof_header prints sizeof(struct fam), which is 4 by derivation on every target, not an
address and not an allocation size.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET. No character data appears anywhere, so C11 5.2.1 leaves nothing here to the
implementation.
THE THREE STORAGE CLASSES ARE THIS PROGRAM'S VARIANT AXIS. The file-scope union is a translation-time
data-section image; its automatic twin -- identical union, identical designated initializer -- must be
materialized by emitted stores on entry to the block; and the allocated object is filled entirely by
emitted stores through the flexible member, with no initializer involved at all. The same syntax
therefore travels three different paths through the compiler. All three are compiled at all three
optimization levels, and no volatile operand is added: qualifying one would suppress exactly the
optimizer behaviour the pairing exists to survive.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at -O0,
-O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 53 lines byte-identically with
exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other without any
narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record. Every live element is printed
individually in the read-back and post-mutation phase of all three objects, so a single wrong slot
localizes to one storage class at one index rather than to the aggregate, and the six sum lines,
together with count_arms_agree and the four count_via_boundary lines, confirm that the same storage is
reached through both union arms and that the allocated object's flexible-member address arithmetic
agrees with itself across a function boundary rather than merely being self-consistent inside main.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
